// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FRONTIER & PHILOSOPHY VOYAGER  |  L11 — The Philosophy of Mind
// Age band : voyagers (11-12)   Guide: orion (Phoenix)
// Standards: Coreverse Original — Philosophy, Philosophy of Mind, Consciousness, Metaphysics
// CALIBRATED: Voyager spec v1.1 (May 2026)
//
// SUBJECT NOTE — Frontier & Philosophy:
//   - The philosophy of mind: what IS consciousness, and how can physical matter produce the
//     felt experience of being someone? Builds directly on L01 (the "hard problem" was named
//     there) and L10 (what science can and can't yet explain).
//   - HONESTY TIER 4 (calibration-critical): the hard problem of consciousness is GENUINELY
//     UNSOLVED. This is one of the most tier-4 lessons in the band. Frame it honestly as an open
//     mystery that brilliant people disagree about — NOT as settled science, and NOT as proof of
//     any particular spiritual claim. The goal is to let a young person feel the genuine awe and
//     difficulty of the question while keeping their footing.
//   - PROTECTIVE BACKBONE (essential here): a real mystery is NOT a license for magical thinking.
//     "Science can't fully explain consciousness yet" does NOT mean "so any claim about
//     consciousness, energy, crystals, or downloading your soul is equally valid." The lesson
//     must hold both: genuine, humbling mystery AND clear standards for what counts as a good
//     answer vs. confident nonsense. This guards the consciousness-curious kid (a real audience)
//     against people who exploit the mystery to sell pseudoscience.
//   - EVENHANDED across the serious positions (materialism, dualism, panpsychism, illusionism,
//     mysterianism); present each at its strongest. Do not push one. Honor wonder without
//     romanticizing or endorsing unfalsifiable spiritual claims as fact.
//   - Term: "philosophical question." Em-dashes natural; do NOT run em-dash replacement.
//
// Interaction format: PERSPECTIVES (screen 7) — DELIBERATE content match, not a default:
// "what is consciousness?" is the textbook competing-positions question. Matches <Perspectives />:
// {id,type,headline,intro,perspectives:[{id,voice,era,stance,quote,argument,evidence,
// strengths_and_limits}],synthesisPrompt,reflectionPrompt}. 5 voices.
//
// QUIZ STANDARD (locked, research-backed): 8 MC + 1 TF + 1 inference. All four MC options SHORT
// (~one line, comparable length), plausible, misconception-based; correct rotated, never the
// obvious longest; rich teaching in `explanation`.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

